WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.410
Here is a real SpecWeave workflow

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.570
in a disposable demo repository.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.730
No production code is touched.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:07.770
First,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.970
create an increment.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.570
Write down the problem,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.010
the acceptance criteria,

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.610
and a task with its exact

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.970
files and test command.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:16.730
Now claim the task,

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.090
so ownership is visible.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.010
Implement the function and run the

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:20.730
tests.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.770
In this example,

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.050
we check a list of numbers

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.210
and an empty list.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.530
Both tests pass.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.210
Commit the implementation.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.450
Then mark the task done with

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.330
the test command.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.650
SpecWeave runs it again and records

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:33.370
the evidence.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.490
To stop,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.370
create an explicit handoff.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.250
This demo uses no push,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:39.810
so everything stays local.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.370
We save that handoff and make

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.050
a clean local clone.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.770
Run pickup in the clone.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.730
The next task is still there:

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.810
document the empty-list behavior.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.610
The plan,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:50.170
ownership,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.810
and evidence travel with the repository.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.250
Try the same workflow in your

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.170
own small project at spec weave

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:57.050
dot com.